WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Christopher Riley, Screenwriter, Author,&nbsp;
and Instructor: His theory says is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.600
no one is smart enough to come&nbsp;
up with 20 bad ideas in a row.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.520
Film Courage: Once a writer has processed their&nbsp;
own defining moments, how do they begin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.120
apply that knowledge to the characters that they&nbsp;
create? Christopher: When I think about applying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.360
the idea of defining moments to characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:32.080
I think in terms of these categories.&nbsp;
If I’ve got a character who has a dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:44.080
I’ve already looked back at my moment of watching&nbsp;
Ordinary People and seeing this dream of somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:52.160
getting my fingers into the clay of filmmaking.&nbsp;
I know about how at least one dream was born,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.600 --> 00:01:01.760
then I can come to my character and say for&nbsp;
this character What does it look like when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:08.240
their dream was born? Now their dream may be&nbsp;
to be an astronaut and so I would think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:18.080
what is analogous from my experience to their&nbsp;
experience? I was watching a movie an astronaut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:25.280
dream might have been born as somebody was&nbsp;
watching a shuttle launch or the tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.400
loss of a shuttle that immediately interests&nbsp;
my writer's brain because it's unexpected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:39.840
that you would see a catastrophic loss of a space&nbsp;
shuttle and say that's what I want to do but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:46.560
I think that's actually often how it&nbsp;
works i've described television writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:54.080
to aspiring writers and the atmosphere in the&nbsp;
writers room and how you have to compete to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:03.600
get your joke on the page and how cutthroat that&nbsp;
can be and I think i've just talked to everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.360
out of wanting to be a television writer and then&nbsp;
people will crowd to the front and say that sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:17.680
fantastic that's exactly what I want to do so&nbsp;
that tells me that sometimes it's even seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:27.360
a story that seems like it would drive you away&nbsp;
that actually attracts the die hard dreamer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:34.480
so I bring that sort of general&nbsp;
experience from life I kind of mix it up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:42.800
with what i've observed in myself&nbsp;
and I will often try to brainstorm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:51.280
a bunch of different options of what that defining&nbsp;
moment might be until I find one that both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:03:00.640
feels emotionally real and also makes an&nbsp;
interesting story because I might have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:09.280
character tell the story or we may flash back to&nbsp;
see that story the writer Bill Marsilii who wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:21.200
the film Déjà Vu talks about his discipline of&nbsp;
brainstorming 20 options for any creative choice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:29.280
and his theory he says is that no one is smart&nbsp;
enough to come up with 20 bad ideas in a row so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:37.440
one of your 20 ideas is bound to be decent so&nbsp;
i've taken to calling that Marsilii’s rule of 20.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:45.280
it is a discipline because often when we're&nbsp;
coming up with say a potential defining moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:52.000
for a character we want to just jump&nbsp;
at the first thing that occurs to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:59.200
part of becoming a mature writer is recognizing&nbsp;
that it's not supposed to be that easy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:06.560
and so we want to stick with it and keep coming up&nbsp;
with options so that we don't pick the first thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.960
that occurs to us which probably is the first&nbsp;
thing that is going to occur to everyone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:18.880
we want to get to something else further down the&nbsp;
line that's unexpected that we haven't seen before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:27.040
and if i'm drawing from my own life experiences&nbsp;
that has a real chance of being fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.400
because it's drawn from life not from the movies

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:39.200
so that would be how I would go about finding&nbsp;
say one defining moment so a dream is born&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.280
maybe in the course of the&nbsp;
film this dream is going to die&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:50.480
so then I would look at my own life and also&nbsp;
lives of people around me that I know well and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.480 --> 00:05:05.920
say where have I seen a dream die and I can think&nbsp;
for myself a defining moment where dreams died and

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:13.520
in my experience dreams die slowly&nbsp;
they die kicking and screaming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.720
we don't want to admit that thing that&nbsp;
you dreamed is not going to happen

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:28.000
and so I would draw on that and say how&nbsp;
do I how do I bring that to this character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:34.480
and I would keep doing that until&nbsp;
I had that handful of moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:40.000
that gives me an understanding&nbsp;
of this character that makes them

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:52.320
complex and relatable and interesting and when I&nbsp;
really I can feel myself when a character starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:58.400
to come alive in this way and I know I want to&nbsp;
spend time with that character and I want to hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:06.560
the dialogue that they're going to say and I want&nbsp;
to see them get into whatever trouble the story is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:12.640
getting them into and I want to see them struggle&nbsp;
to get out of that trouble to get to their goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:18.320
and I also want to watch them grow&nbsp;
over the course of that story either&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.080
for good or for ill they're going to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:30.320
and they're going to change in in these moments&nbsp;
these pivot points and so I just keep at it

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:44.720
until I feel like I have this full character it's&nbsp;
it's an organic messy process definitely not paint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:53.040
by the numbers but having my own history&nbsp;
and an understanding of myself to draw on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:59.200
also having this sort of set of&nbsp;
characteristics of defining moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:08.000
helps me at least know where to look and then&nbsp;
I just I try to keep looking and until I found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.000
what I need to find it's interesting because&nbsp;
we're talking about defining moments for various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.560
characters and knowing them sometimes if i've&nbsp;
watched a biopic I feel as if the filmmaker has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:25.840
tried to cram in so many defining moments that it&nbsp;
loses some of the impact of them and I think it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:31.600
because there's so much to tell yeah but then i've&nbsp;
seen documentaries where they also do the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:37.520
and for some reason it lands better I don't know&nbsp;
why that would be I don't know I do think that&nbsp;

00:07:40.400 --> 00:07:47.040
the best biopics tend to focus on a single&nbsp;
event I think of Spielberg's Lincoln&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:57.600
and we know so much of Abraham Lincoln's life&nbsp;
and career and Spielberg and Tony Kushner the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:06.480
screenwriter chose to focus on just a single&nbsp;
vote in the US House of Representatives about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:12.560
the Amendment to the Constitution that outlawed&nbsp;
slavery and so out of everything you could look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:24.080
at in Lincoln's life and career the film focuses&nbsp;
on a single event and I think we get a bunch more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.640 --> 00:08:34.240
dramatic power focused when when we do that&nbsp;
and it can be really tempting if you're doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:41.520
a biopic because so many interesting things&nbsp;
happened in in many people's lives if we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:49.520
doing Winston Churchill there'd be so many things&nbsp;
to focus on but the fantastic film darkest hour&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:59.840
focuses very specifically on the couple of&nbsp;
weeks leading up to Winston Churchill's speech&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:04.800
to parliament where he declares we're going&nbsp;
to fight on the beaches we're going to fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:11.360
on the landing field where he galvanizes&nbsp;
the British people to oppose the Nazis.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:24.960
That is a dramatic move to to restrict yourself&nbsp;
in that way and at the risk of sounding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:34.640
disgustingly like a film professor I'll go back&nbsp;
to Aristotle one more time and Aristotle as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:40.720
long ago as that said that the&nbsp;
drama should be about a single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:46.320
event and I think that I think&nbsp;
he's right and I think that um

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:59.600
for reasons I don't understand we are we are&nbsp;
geared toward a story of a hero struggling against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:09.360
obstacles to reach a goal there is a unity to&nbsp;
drama that both focuses its power but also helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:18.960
to give it meaning because there's a there's a&nbsp;
configuration of events that becomes meaningful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:26.880
these choices and these actions led to this&nbsp;
outcome so that's it gives us this sense of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:35.600
completion that I think we was one of the&nbsp;
things that we long for from our stories.

